  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 235 AM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThe aforementioned inverted trough is expected to lift northward \nalong the Florida Peninsula late Friday into Saturday. Deep tropical \nmoisture (PWATs generally exceeding 2 inches) will linger across \nSouth Florida through much of Saturday, supporting enhanced \nconvective coverage focused across the interior and southwest \nregions. As the disturbance lifts north of the area, Atlantic \nsurface high pressure will gradually reestablish itself across the \nFlorida Peninsula. This will maintain a moderate easterly to \nsoutheasterly flow through early next week before the pressure \ngradient relaxes somewhat by the middle of the week, resulting in a \nlighter broad easterly flow. \n\nModel and ensemble guidance remain in good agreement depicting a \ngradual drying trend beginning late this weekend and continuing into \nearly next week, with PWAT values generally decreasing into the 1.6 \nto 1.8 inch range. These moisture levels are closer to seasonal \nnorms and sufficient to support typical summertime convection. \nAccordingly, scattered afternoon and evening showers are expected \neach day through the extended period, with the greatest coverage \ngenerally favoring the interior and southwest Florida under the \nprevailing easterly flow. A plume of Saharan dust may approach \nSouth Florida by the middle of next week, which could suppress \noverall convective coverage and intensity, but confidence on its \nimpacts remains low at this time. \n\nThe return of Atlantic ridging and overall reduction in cloud \ncover will support a slow warming trend through the extended \nforecast period. Afternoon high temperatures are expected to climb\ninto the mid 90s across much of South Florida, while peak heat \nindices may reach 105 to 110 F each afternoon. Moderate HeatRisk \nwill persist, especially across the east coast metro and southwest\nFlorida. This warming trend will continue to be monitored closely\nover the coming days for the potential of increasing heat-related\nimpacts across the region.",
